#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523} :activity/id #uuid "757509db-ea62-5c97-af42-cffc3637196f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85"} :step/id #uuid "2774fb5d-55a7-5428-8581-814bde185950"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193823 :tutorial/content "Recall that the principal components of a standardized observation matrix $X$ are the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$ If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nFor example, suppose that the covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is \n\n![](images/e4/e40cd1a0df89ba171b921ab971e8eee25e284265df13b7e031ea32fc28d2c27b.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show that the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=10, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the first principal component of the data, we need the unit eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=10.$ \n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, seeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/c7/c7050bbb395b939b1b0559b3e7515c4be4f572aed61090f1a4489e126763240f.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/7b/7b0a41deb385aa2471cae701c9f87938085256879a8d834535f8ec0e3f431f27.svg) \n\nTherefore, the $1$st principal component of the data is ![](images/94/9490f2e5105592fa3976ae506cbdf64d70c3372d1cc9295eaa6c6580a4ac75aa.svg)\n\n**Note:** The opposite vector ![](images/e2/e2b366cfb8cb5b708eb5ba443dfc61c05b498d4eb737b375c8ebc86b96e8eea6.svg) also works in the example above." :tutorial/id #uuid "a97acfe0-1488-53e4-bcb3-6876fe2ce042" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6507 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c43bb8da-171f-50ea-8f50-5beb61c94790" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730"} :step/id #uuid "2b9d675a-327f-591d-9bc2-fa8d6b6ff934" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193502}}] :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192865} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534141} :activity/id #uuid "6ead580b-4ede-5c00-946f-c5a855bc1388" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192964 :question/id #uuid "d35740ea-ab16-5dbd-b830-35948c9ebd0c"} :step/id #uuid "a3410547-abc5-50e3-b02f-0dc8ed29d6e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533999 :question/id #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4"} :step/id #uuid "b50ea736-e70c-587d-b358-26135009e6d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533994 :question/id #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9"} :step/id #uuid "cdf898f6-45d1-58e0-a005-fd32969067d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534047 :question/id #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2"} :step/id #uuid "e74a8af4-059f-50bf-807d-00656676699e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534045 :question/id #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca"} :step/id #uuid "ed7a7cf8-ffdb-5b0f-9201-cd08ad8f2b59" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534152}}] :activity/title "Computing Principal Components" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533124}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048721 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192623 :question/id #uuid "1ffdc04e-8224-542d-80f5-99322b693ccf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9836" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n![](images/f5/f51f3460981ca243627f1873a24fa3a50a43275bebdb20c1ced5d64215c71618.svg)\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $S$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=8,\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=5,\\qquad\n\\lambda_3=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=8{:}$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-8I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/eb/eb25f50c2550e470fcdffd1c94a5ff2134043ac34b6a75b14e2ce00d1936a2e8.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| =3,$ we get \n\n![](images/92/920ccfb9953cf4e17f89b01a1f5512f8c0bbcc368f9af1770f768c1e258127e5.svg) \n\nTherefore, the first principal component of the data is ![](images/0c/0c20bbbce9a9014103e94d0f8b055f6dfec18672f2b313d46b1799d9402f755a.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7674fd32-6462-5973-9b40-930637b68c85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caf9374-a8b9-56f8-9a51-1f6c33377318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513609} {:answer/id #uuid "478cdd6a-a11c-5ded-9658-0ec619567053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513724} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f028a0-351b-53d6-95ad-6d6df0bd24cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514025} {:answer/id #uuid "cf081a75-0c42-57a1-9dee-9c14a25e2410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514073} {:answer/id #uuid "d55415e6-163a-508c-8ecf-e8fbc0a226b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514090} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ebf47f9-948f-5a15-bd99-d2bd7bdca3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189676}] :question/id #uuid "bff16bd8-7b45-5bbd-8528-7bc476ccdc82" :question/math-academy-id "q-137740" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10,$ $5,$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d16cf009-3eb0-5602-b389-13e763c9dc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189695} {:answer/id #uuid "e40bb5bc-d04f-55a8-a043-da35a773e56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189696} {:answer/id #uuid "04f2506f-e274-5a37-8b2e-1f76ba17549e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189697} {:answer/id #uuid "0780e87d-47d4-55e0-aff9-0865998123f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{21}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189698} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9bba80-7b2f-5fbf-a266-b6fb9bb66ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189699}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4b33225-cd21-503d-8356-08230ad0f660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189694}] :question/id #uuid "5c680b52-66b5-521d-8c28-5e047c1a513b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138519" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below.\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $6$, $3$, and $0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30a3113b-fc17-596b-9f95-def6091c6999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513660} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae9e2d9-02b8-5bf3-9e21-3b0ccae3f4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513774} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6b67ad-416f-59d6-a159-dc4add175bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513854} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0c7856-5a8b-5fd6-87db-61cf81268afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513975} {:answer/id #uuid "d24bafaa-3b7e-53f5-9a86-3e1dc5279c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513774} :answer-field/id #uuid "145dd816-7538-567d-8cf5-d59c4f788fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514221}] :question/id #uuid "1dfd23ff-dd1b-5e4d-b783-10b5e0f038d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149404" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $4,$ and $8.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216c564c-5ad0-50d5-982d-6e8432fadef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513622} {:answer/id #uuid "570890c9-81bc-575a-a7d3-d1c395f52180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513768} {:answer/id #uuid "5c298372-518e-51e5-8af5-1a96d417af2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513780} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3241c0-aebf-5f17-b035-18257b7ec1f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513833} {:answer/id #uuid "c355b21c-fab5-547e-b193-a8f5ac6b906a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514048} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e70a197-b3ce-5e88-b246-d5ee3922e949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514229}] :question/id #uuid "30659c57-f541-5ec5-857d-e24511eb8172" :question/math-academy-id "q-149430" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $3,$ and $9.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f26650b-f307-5083-ac8d-c889e76cf7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513656} {:answer/id #uuid "6770be34-175a-5663-8664-d993ad4bebbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513809} {:answer/id #uuid "af67ddbf-eada-5914-aaaa-00feff03ccc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513992} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb15a69-5ab5-567e-872d-498d5e6ae6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514120} {:answer/id #uuid "f66f6760-690a-575d-8e8a-2c912554b5d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513992} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a98c77-18cd-5480-9aa3-b3e4273a0328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514249}] :question/id #uuid "5374b353-43b9-56d5-bf37-798eca076a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-138531" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6efbdee7-7d3a-5707-9520-c57843d2fe06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513832} {:answer/id #uuid "89d016a3-becd-5d35-807d-bea843a21d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513890} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c0e02-bfc6-53c8-ba64-3ec942a9b78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513932} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b50652-4f11-5f47-9388-bfd66be24cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514077} {:answer/id #uuid "dc322190-b60c-5ac0-bb33-83b62a57c1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514110} :answer-field/id #uuid "283a2bc1-2dc2-54df-b080-f5e691fb529a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514239}] :question/id #uuid "5ddc6be0-7f0e-552b-88a6-224cbf5ee88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138521" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $5,$ $4,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533155} {:answer/id #uuid "482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533306} {:answer/id #uuid "9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533511} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533631} {:answer/id #uuid "e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533306} :answer-field/id #uuid "105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533787}] :question/id #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138515" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533245} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533619} {:answer/id #uuid "cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533645} {:answer/id #uuid "d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533665} {:answer/id #uuid "f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533747} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533782}] :question/id #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138532" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 3x3 Covariance Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192401 :question/id #uuid "9f2942ee-4d9f-5436-a021-9e325bfc694b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10450" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n![](images/78/7839eb7b2cfa2511661a6f2822930cd54ef4c3f2cb7df53448af61bdb7f63766.svg)\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $16$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 \\gt \\lambda_2 \\gt \\cdots\\gt \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_1$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue $\\lambda_1,$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf u_2$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue $\\lambda_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are $\\lambda_1=16$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=6{:}$\n\nApplying our usual algorithm for finding eigenvectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/e5/e5ce684566df94e2d3691bb3fd75b78e46bbfe20bc3ed037618175c619cbe89a.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n![](images/10/10818d19ffec73c77257977086f97924f8c3ff35fdb4cb46a1a84fa243bbcdbb.svg) \n\nTherefore, the second principal component of the data is ![](images/3c/3cfd9b938a757c8e5b06ebf426b71e0439175e862da98ac7e65e74ee413f2d68.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40fb56dd-7e99-5cb6-a3f6-f9ddd630c730" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533176} {:answer/id #uuid "293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533244} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533438} {:answer/id #uuid "ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533553} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533176} :answer-field/id #uuid "310815ed-9218-576f-8607-844dee4ec567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189670}] :question/id #uuid "d35740ea-ab16-5dbd-b830-35948c9ebd0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-137739" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6874ad-3005-516e-a0ad-5cb17e1bb218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513569} {:answer/id #uuid "4c15a98b-50e4-52bd-a670-8083176f53dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513740} {:answer/id #uuid "77c2fdcd-3cfd-5138-b031-10ea6cb25796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513848} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ae3c3a-bf66-58f6-85cd-2aa0368d5c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513967} {:answer/id #uuid "d2934599-3ad3-591c-91b5-c462470674f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e265595c-4439-5c82-a9e6-7d73e24470a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189688}] :question/id #uuid "37adab1d-55b2-5b32-adbd-422291307785" :question/math-academy-id "q-138512" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $12$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=12$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c573d-7ae8-5596-961d-d21ec88bd4d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513713} {:answer/id #uuid "64df2e0c-e2f2-597f-