2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2146da66-b60d-5beb-a83b-603030a5d8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513621} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a9875-89ac-5fc5-9906-3cd3164d6d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513648} {:answer/id #uuid "592663a7-2308-5850-bedd-0fb5f3ee0ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513771} {:answer/id #uuid "87935c1d-3718-5f26-8460-6ca7631017a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513886} {:answer/id #uuid "b85fd2dd-6759-5b78-9ba7-4715c2d8f37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513771} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ce8fe7-2fa0-5374-84b1-d90a49b96890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514219}] :question/id #uuid "95e787d7-1400-508c-9805-e2c36a0d2bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-149395" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $17$ and $7.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=17$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 16\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-7I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uui