 order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03883e00-ff01-5a79-b12f-032b887736a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513538} {:answer/id #uuid "058a06a8-4305-5b98-8e52-81f40a72b383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513543} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa9504b-805c-5abe-a81a-22e86efad5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513615} {:answer/id #uuid "51578d0d-37f6-594d-8ac9-09733a9ba993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513754} {:answer/id #uuid "630b44dc-a913-5dc9-9613-2dda312da9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513615} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f8a878-942e-5a59-993e-ff2e07b1ea9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514261}] :question/id #uuid "e7322fc5-138d-596a-9e83-0b51cc3f5715" :question/math-academy-id "q-138505" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $4$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533215} {:answer/id #uuid "2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533222} {:answer/id #uuid "26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533236} {:answer/id #uuid "a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533546} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533570} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533910}] :question/id #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138510" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533195} {:answer/id #uuid "2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533250} {:answer/id #uuid "756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533418} {:answer/id #uuid "af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533562} {:answer/id #uuid "ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533195} :answer-field/id #uuid "44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533834}] :question/id #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138506" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Principal Component Given a 2x2 Covariance Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3945 :topic/title "Computing Principal Components"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429} :activity/id #uuid "f4d9b1d3-776c-5de2-b62c-cbb016b96f1e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125794 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of a square matrix $A$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the left-hand side of this equation, we have the expression \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I),\n$$\n\n which we call the **characteristic polynomial** of the matrix $A.$\n\nFor instance, let's find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix ![](images/1d/1d6d0bdedbb25bbc41759e443cdc0f6a207ef5cbfa4761b05e1a1a2eda24cbec.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(C-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =(-8-\\lambda )\\cdot (7-\\lambda )-(-9)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25396b96-19ec-592d-b1c4-3c6fa172ef08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3580 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9083e01a-4e57-589b-9b08-48ecc4af0075" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125795 :tutorial/content "The **Cayley-Hamilton theorem** states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $\\,n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix whose characteristic polynomial is* \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) = (-1)^n\\lambda^n + c_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1\\lambda + c_0.\n$$\n\n*Then, the matrix $A$ is a solution of the characteristic equation $p(\\lambda) = 0$.* \n\nThat is to say, if we substitute $A$ into the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$, taking $A^0 = I,$ then we have\n\n$$\np(A) = (-1)^nA^n + c_{n-1}A^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1A + c_0I = O,\n$$\n\nwhere $O$ is the zero-matrix. \n\nFor example, according to this theorem, for the matrix \n![](images/d2/d24f480293267a9ddc28b03626e7bde6f01bd62a136fed17f8580bf1ea34d776.svg)\nwhose characteristic polynomial is \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2 +\\lambda -2,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 +A -2I =O.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86101838-75af-5a3f-9dae-1e63a94c77a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3581 :tutorial/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7b3b4f95-8159-5533-bd80-db194716f75b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7"} :step/id #uuid "d9e0bce9-63bf-58e4-af9b-2bb5b5d8e03c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38"} :step/id #uuid "1c6e6482-d051-50c1-b91d-40d9a95a380d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c54b7a6-10ea-5c57-ad4e-245a308d4790" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3582 :tutorial/title "The Characteristic Polynomial for 2x2 Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "4e500723-739c-51c7-b82e-8727a24a1b1f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8"} :step/id #uuid "0fed3d43-139d-505f-8482-70f3be0516d6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57"} :step/id #uuid "f3faf291-dd20-56c9-bc3d-8482113b6764" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125824 :tutorial/content "If we want to evaluate a high-degree polynomial for a given matrix input, it can take a lot of time. For example, suppose we wanted to evaluate the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^{10} - 5x^9 - 2x^8 + x + 2\n$$\n\nfor the input ![](images/0f/0f9bff826261a956305962b4ca945ed91012cc5042e6ffe92a4b5f1ce13d3237.svg) Computing the $10$th power of this matrix would be a lot of work!\n\nLuckily, there is a way to speed up the process. The trick is to use Cayley-Hamilton theorem, which tells us that every matrix is a solution to its characteristic equation.\n\nFor the given matrix ![](images/65/6514096b22aed47005e5ff59c47e3cec701aed5b120b0022b186f1bec941d953.svg) the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the Cayley-Hamilton theorem tells us that\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 - 5A - 2I = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, observe that the polynomial $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{10}-5{x}^{9}-2{x}^{8}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}({x}^{2}-5x-2)+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}p(x)+x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = A^8 p(A) + A + 2I,\n$$\n\nand because $p(A) = 0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{8}\\cdot 0+A+2I \\\\  & =0+A+2I \\\\  & =A+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the computation is much faster:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =A+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it may not be so obvious how to group the terms of $f(x)$ to reveal $p(x).$ In the general case, it's best to divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division, and use the result\n\n$$\nf(x) = q(x) p(x) + r(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "769ceb10-1667-52b6-8a89-b4c7ca835276" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4448 :tutorial/title "Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem to Quickly Evaluate a Matrix Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "3167e19f-42e7-5fc7-8ee9-fb73a5fa3da3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434}}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124757} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844} :activity/id #uuid "08986419-9a71-537f-9305-f8e778b26413" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446 :question/id #uuid "83ac805a-2248-5c38-94ec-8370eb490b39"} :step/id #uuid "37974a8d-1c28-54c7-b296-b5b3c2e14d09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443 :question/id #uuid "03d6fc6a-0f72-5d35-ba67-c4e4d25be4dc"} :step/id #uuid "864708dc-01cf-5025-81a5-3d649155dedc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440842}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c"} :step/id #uuid "8b979659-38ec-5258-8754-0f5aa34455d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451 :question/id #uuid "e1de5236-83e9-5753-abb0-71abaf264e50"} :step/id #uuid "9ee92d90-8ec9-5c83-abd0-9bf2b158360d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142 :question/id #uuid "af2db92b-ede0-52d7-a7d4-1043a63d9db0"} :step/id #uuid "a1707eef-06e0-5503-8342-6b9421462ee5"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440841} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487} :activity/id #uuid "335bb2d0-e529-5cbe-ad74-695b640df1d6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811 :question/id #uuid "0d383f46-698f-5924-8d8d-4466dfa087b0"} :step/id #uuid "29abcc68-95f8-5957-a518-0a35c26f28c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140 :question/id #uuid "241347e1-d70b-502b-97bb-5ffa2634f0a1"} :step/id #uuid "4d30d155-c0af-5612-bddd-5c0adca752a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940 :question/id #uuid "a72555f5-f964-563d-a2c6-2e5bad5e5508"} :step/id #uuid "712962aa-1f8a-5106-8afe-8f1d72596893" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225 :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57"} :step/id #uuid "aa57e7bc-ae6a-50ca-b096-5a8dfd3e3450" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486439 :question/id #uuid "c746a2db-cfe0-519b-a0f3-936f85b88bb1"} :step/id #uuid "f73f0739-b0b6-5a0b-9fac-ab5fdff3e3e1"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485755} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562572} :activity/id #uuid "429544ed-8bbc-5289-a093-2f183882bb3e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354445 :question/id #uuid "e3d8a5b7-428e-5b9f-a099-8d78abc215c6"} :step/id #uuid "009b2545-43e5-5219-8e67-bc5239b87a2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392218 :question/id #uuid "6884fbdf-c141-559a-9c69-73ca1eb1083a"} :step/id #uuid "577e82f8-0c54-5def-a845-15ce9bcbcc0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/id #uuid "688a2834-9fcd-5275-97a0-97e7aaa1b79c"} :step/id #uuid "c9488e67-2747-5299-bfa1-6e5c456d8b59" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140 :question/id #uuid "241347e1-d70b-502b-97bb-5ffa2634f0a1"} :step/id #uuid "d76a9bcf-ce1d-59e1-a458-d42736b3c5ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451 :question/id #uuid "e1de5236-83e9-5753-abb0-71abaf264e50"} :step/id #uuid "e5720d7a-1ab9-5e71-a410-d953677a5677"}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561565}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/2e/2e4a7f95853bbfc023f9f4be8278063ed945889e84fd6b2bc2100a22dbd90994.svg) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad1c106-e555-5ab5-a741-a4cdc7c69178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321234} {:answer/id #uuid "34a01fff-5e6b-50b0-9a81-d354a982151b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321548} {:answer/id #uuid "5c921480-996f-547f-b72f-7f34613e006b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321880} {:answer/id #uuid "80f9676b-bed6-5d2f-bf33-5b0764fda6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322161} {:answer/id #uuid "9dcf1a38-313b-56b9-aaf8-6ce81f6c07cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321880} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f66c2c3-662d-5a6c-b51b-54dc28c47dab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323447}] :question/id #uuid "4acccd18-c797-5d41-ab58-67f98662f9b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37779" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-8A+9I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-8A+9I) & =O \\\\ A-8I+9{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{8}{9}I-\\frac{1}{9}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{8}{9},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{9},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{8}{9}+(-\\frac{1}{9})=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544109} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I+A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544286} {:answer/id #uuid "906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} {:answer/id #uuid "9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I+3A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544423} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3I+9A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3364b42-0269-5bbd-853e-2a760baddc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123188}] :question/id #uuid "1aab1c27-1f53-564d-b691-068d98d1a09c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37780" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )^{2}-15) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-9{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-9A+9I-{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =9I-9A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a94ae5e-84f5-5fe7-9219-3b799a5dab21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}I+\\frac{1}{3}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269801} {:answer/id #uuid "62ae8d57-cee3-56ab-b4cf-5f863ad27aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} {:answer/id #uuid "7563f626-e047-55a1-8e63-8641f45069f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I-\\frac{1}{6}A+\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270306} {:answer/id #uuid "ad282852-264a-521a-92c0-2b9671da7fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{12}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270812} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f15110-7592-5194-9248-7f09143aaa7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{6}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} :answer-field/id #uuid "1747bf63-e28c-57b2-a5d7-9c5c047b1742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271548}] :question/id #uuid "b990092f-3b4e-5607-8795-8e6af5838a33" :question/math-academy-id "q-140531" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-2A-11I+24{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 24{A}^{-1} & =11I+2A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d94a386-0e3f-5fd6-82d2-7e3b9d7cbeec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc3e40-f109-58b8-872a-95626283c9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321493} {:answer/id #uuid "389d0b0e-b88a-5ab8-9fa3-85ae37139eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321578} {:answer/id #uuid "73fc0bbe-4d33-5b94-bad1-7a748598633e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322053} {:answer/id #uuid "f9936d7e-e12d-5d02-a132-90f3f19d81e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252} :answer-field/id #uuid "399304a9-7f1c-5273-8e27-ae2b68d805f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323284}] :question/id #uuid "a72555f5-f964-563d-a2c6-2e5bad5e5508" :question/math-academy-id "q-37778" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-4A+I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-4A+I) & =O \\\\ A-4I+{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =4I-A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4,$ $q=-1$ and\n\n$$\np+q=4+(-1)=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58cd8c10-e2a6-5947-bc5e-bd7252f874ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2I+4A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321845} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef11ac5-425d-5221-81cd-bb0301dba0f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{4}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322146} {:answer/id #uuid "a89c0dd5-8c0f-5539-a8f4-1967c175fd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322469} {:answer/id #uuid "c852b2c1-f95c-553c-bb44-facc9b6e051b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5I-4A-5{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322701} {:answer/id #uuid "fa90974d-526a-5239-92f5-037f6ab63f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{1}{5}A+\\frac{2}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322469} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a583b8-3d3b-5d9c-8a9f-6dc754bba588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323399}] :question/id #uuid "5734eef3-bee7-5a34-ab88-0e69bc193d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37831" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+1) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+2{A}^{2}-4A-5I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+2A-4I-5{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{4}{5}I+\\frac{2}{5}A+\\frac{1}{5}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28cc5ee2-6da9-52c1-a92b-8edb26634139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300} {:answer/id #uuid "47e93b3f-33d7-5090-b1fc-02844e927bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324553} {:answer/id #uuid "a44579f4-a9e4-53da-a506-9eefbb4bf2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325367} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f6f17-d42e-553d-a74b-0e76268b5611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325601} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b95922-aad1-5b77-a0d0-e6a698ab713b